This contract is a services contract within the meaning of Article 2, 21°, of the law of 17 June 2016. It covers coaching, strategic support and organisational development services for the benefit of public administrations. It aims to support administrations in an integrated organisational approach regarding the definition, implementation and management of their strategic orientations, as well as the strengthening of their organisation, governance, managerial practices, resource management and stakeholder mobilisation. It helps to ensure the alignment of the various levels of governance and management around defined objectives, including in the context of change, transformation or crisis, in order to guarantee coherent and shared deployment of strategies. This contract is divided into lots as follows: STRATEGIC PLANNING AND ORGANISATIONAL RESILIENCE • Lot 1: Support for the preparation of the cross-cutting strategic plan (CSP) • Lot 2: Support for resource planning and optimisation • Lot 3: Support for the revision of the organisational framework and the organisation chart incorporating a forward-looking job and skills management (FLSM) approach • Lot 4: Support for the preparation of a business continuity plan (BCP) within an integrated crisis management approach STRATEGY DEPLOYMENT AND MANAGEMENT • Lot 5: Support for project portfolio management • Lot 6: Change management • Lot 7: Support for internal and external communication GOVERNANCE, MANAGEMENT AND TEAM DYNAMICS • Lot 8: Support for management and direction committees • Lot 9: Individual coaching for managers • Lot 10: Team support Target group: Brussels local authorities (municipalities and CPAS) as well as bodies carrying out public utility missions within the Brussels-Capital Region. SUPPORT FOR THE PREPARATION OF THE CROSS-CUTTING STRATEGIC PLAN ERAP supports public administrations in defining, structuring and implementing their strategic vision. This support aims to assist local authorities in drawing up their Cross-Cutting Strategic Plan (CSP) and in the operational translation of political orientations. The scheme includes in particular: • Organisational diagnosis and identification of strategic challenges; • Definition of fundamentals: mission, vision, values; • Development of the strategy and its implementation plan; • Validation, communication and dissemination of the CSP; • Definition of monitoring, management and evaluation procedures. The interventions are aimed at political representatives, secretaries, management committees and managers involved in the strategic process.; TEAM SUPPORT The transformations facing public administrations require the development of new forms of cooperation, collective responsibility and organisational agility. ERAP supports teams in the evolution of their operating methods in order to strengthen their collective effectiveness and capacity for adaptation. Interventions may focus in particular on: • Strengthening cohesion and cooperation; • Improving working and collaboration practices; • The evolution of collective responsibility; • Regulation of organisational or relational difficulties; • Support for transformation projects; • Sustainable strengthening of collective performance. The support is aimed at operational teams, departments and work collectives involved in transformation or continuous improvement dynamics.; SUPPORT FOR RESOURCE PLANNING AND OPTIMISATION In a context of increasing budgetary constraints and changing citizen needs, public administrations must strengthen their capacity to plan and optimise the use of their resources. ERAP supports organisations in a structured approach aimed at ensuring alignment between missions, available resources and strategic orientations. The support includes in particular: • Analysis of territorial developments and future needs; • Analysis of activities, processes and organisational methods; • Identification of critical skills and development needs; • Identification of efficiency levers, in particular through expenditure optimisation, the development of organisational synergies, the pooling of resources, skills and infrastructure, the search for economies of scale as well as process simplification; • Preparation and evaluation of evolution and optimisation scenarios in order to anticipate their organisational, human and financial impacts and inform decision-making. This approach is carried out in close collaboration with political and administrative authorities to ensure consistency between strategic orientations and their operational implementation.; SUPPORT FOR THE REVISION OF THE FRAMEWORK AND ORGANISATION CHART ERAP supports public administrations in adapting their organisational framework and organisation chart to ensure they align with strategic orientations and mission developments. This support aims to ensure coherent structuring of the organisation, promoting clarity of roles, responsibilities and reporting lines as well as smooth internal operations. It may focus in particular on: • the analysis of the existing organisation and its alignment with strategic objectives; • the definition or revision of the organisation chart;

• the clarification of responsibilities, missions and scopes of action; • the identification of structural adjustment needs linked to strategic or operational developments; • support for the implementation of reorganisations. Interventions are aimed at political authorities, general managements and executive committees in order to support a structured and coherent evolution of the public organisation.; SUPPORT FOR THE DEVELOPMENT OF A BUSINESS CONTINUITY PLAN (BCP) IN AN INTEGRATED CRISIS MANAGEMENT APPROACH Public administrations operate in an environment marked by growing risks linked to geopolitical crises, climate change, energy tensions, cyber threats and other events likely to disrupt the continuity of public services. In this context, ERAP supports organisations in developing their organisational resilience and strengthening their business continuity arrangements. Support includes in particular: • The definition of the objectives and scope of the BCP; • The identification of stakeholders and their responsibilities; • The analysis of the organisational environment, missions, critical activities and risks; • The assessment of potential impacts linked to a service interruption; • The identification and prioritisation of critical activities; • The identification of resources necessary for the continuity of essential activities; • The definition of organisational and operational continuity measures; • The development of operational support materials facilitating crisis management and service continuity. Interventions are aimed at political authorities, general managers, members of executive committees, service managers as well as all stakeholders involved in business continuity, risk management and crisis management, in order to guarantee a coordinated and shared approach to organisational resilience.; SUPPORT FOR PROJECT PORTFOLIO MANAGEMENT Public administrations simultaneously manage numerous transformation projects in complex and constrained contexts. It is essential to ensure the consistency of initiatives, their alignment with strategic directions and optimal resource allocation. ERAP supports organisations in structuring, steering and dynamically adapting their project portfolio. This support includes in particular: • The establishment of portfolio governance; • The prioritisation and arbitration of projects according to strategic issues and operational constraints; • Monitoring of progress, results and risks; • The adaptation of priorities according to the changing context; • The optimisation of resource allocation; • The development of management tools; • Alignment between projects, activities and strategic directions. These interventions concern public administrations and are aimed at political authorities, general managements, executive committees as well as managers and teams involved in strategic steering, project management and the implementation of organisational transformations.; CHANGE MANAGEMENT Organisational transformations imply profound evolutions in working practices, operating methods and professional cultures. Their success relies on the organisation's capacity to mobilise the actors concerned and support transitions. ERAP supports public administrations in change management using a methodological approach. This support includes in particular: • The analysis of organisational, human and operational impacts; • The identification and mobilisation of stakeholders; • The implementation of appropriate and continuous communication; • Supporting teams through transition phases; • The development of skills necessary for the appropriation of changes; • The implementation of actions favouring buy-in, commitment and empowerment; • Monitoring the appropriation of changes over time. Change management aims to secure transformations, strengthen team mobilisation and guarantee the sustainable anchoring of new practices within the organisation.; SUPPORT FOR INTERNAL AND EXTERNAL COMMUNICATION Communication constitutes an essential lever for the functioning of public organisations, both in the circulation of internal information and in relations with citizens and users of public services. In a context of organisational transformation and changing user expectations, it is important to have consistent, legible communication practices adapted to different audiences. ERAP supports public administrations in strengthening their internal and external communication practices, in connection with issues of governance, management and quality of public service. In this framework, ERAP has developed a scheme for upskilling communication managers, notably through a cycle of masterclasses in strategic communication entitled "STRATCOM". This scheme aims to support strategic reflection and the development of professional practices adapted to the challenges of public organisations. This support forms part of the continuation of this dynamic and aims to support the concrete implementation of communication strategies and practices within organisations. This support may notably focus on: • The analysis of existing communication practices and arrangements; • The clarification and structuring of messages; • The improvement of internal information circulation;

• Strengthening the consistency of managerial and organisational communications; • Supporting communications related to projects and transformations; • Improving the legibility of information intended for citizens and users; • Support for institutional communication and interactions with the public; • The development of communication practices adapted to different audiences. Interventions are aimed at political authorities, general managements, executive committees, management teams as well as all actors involved in the internal and external communication of the organisation.; SUPPORT FOR EXECUTIVE COMMITTEES AND MANAGEMENT Beyond the executive committee, the hierarchical line, also called depending on the administration extended executive committee, management team or management teams, occupies a key position in the implementation of strategic decisions. It acts as a relay between the vision carried by the executive committee and its operational translation within services. As such, it contributes to spreading the organisational managerial culture, strengthening the consistency of management practices, mobilising teams and supporting daily changes. ERAP supports executive committees, extended executive committees as well as management teams in order to strengthen their collective efficiency, steering capacity and their role in the deployment of strategic directions. Interventions may notably focus on: • The development of a shared strategic vision; • Strengthening cohesion and collective intelligence; • The improvement of decision-making processes; • The evolution of governance and management practices; • The development of leadership and a culture of collaboration; • Strengthening managerial alignment and consistency of practices within the hierarchical line; • Supporting the implementation of strategic directions and transformation projects; • The development of communication, mobilisation and team support capacities. Interventions are aimed at members of executive committees, extended executive committees, department heads, service heads as well as all management and steering structures.; INDIVIDUAL COACHING FOR MANAGERS Managers play an essential role in implementing guidelines, supporting teams and driving transformations, in constantly evolving professional contexts. ERAP offers individual coaching support, tailor-made according to each situation and the specific needs of the manager. This support offers a space for stepping back, reflection and support in the exercise of managerial responsibilities. It is part of the reality of the manager's role, marked by multiple responsibilities, regular arbitration and, sometimes, a positioning that can lead to a certain isolation in decision-making and change management. This support may notably focus on: • The development of leadership and managerial posture; • The quality of communication and working relationships; • Decision-making and priority management; • Supporting collaborators; • The management of change situations and complex contexts; • The development of the capacity for detachment and discernment. Support measures are individualised and aimed at managers, service heads, team leaders as well as any person exercising a supervisory or coordination function.
